"""Twelve original sickle-cell questions appended to the hemolysis chapter."""
from create_hemolytic_anemia import rows,q,WA,AI,SU,HS,G6,CP,PK,BA
from qbank_authoring import ref
SC=ref('NHLBI: Evidence-Based Sickle Cell Disease Management','https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/sites/default/files/media/docs/Evd-Bsd_SickleCellDis_Rep2014.pdf')
TR=ref('ASH: Sickle Cell Disease Transfusion Support Guideline','https://ashpublications.org/bloodadvances/article/4/2/327/440607/American-Society-of-Hematology-2020-guidelines-for')
VO=ref('FDA: Voxelotor Withdrawal due to Safety Concerns','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fda-alerting-patients-and-health-care-professionals-about-voluntary-withdrawal-oxbryta-market-due')
GE=ref('FDA: Casgevy Mechanism and Regulatory Review','https://www.fda.gov/media/175842/download')
q('What initiates red-cell sickling in HbSS disease?', 'Polymerization of deoxygenated HbS',[
 ('An antibody binding to the Rh antigen','That is an immune mechanism rather than the primary HbS defect.'),('A primary spectrin deficiency','That causes a membrane disorder, not the HbS polymerization mechanism.'),('Failure of intrinsic-factor production','That is a B12 absorption mechanism.')],
 'A structural globin change produces an oxygen-dependent polymerization tendency', '**Deoxygenated HbS can form polymers that distort the red cell.**',['Cell dehydration and repeated cycling worsen injury.','Hemolysis, adhesion and inflammation contribute to vascular complications.'], '**Treat both acute triggers and chronic disease risk.**',['Avoid hypoxemia, dehydration and other precipitating stresses.','Disease modification reduces more than the visible sickle shape on a smear.'],[['Molecular event','Deoxygenated HbS polymerization'],['Cell effect','Sickling and membrane injury'],['Clinical effects','Hemolysis and vaso-occlusion']],[SC])
q('A patient with HbSS develops severe limb pain typical of prior vaso-occlusive episodes, with no acute chest, stroke or other complication. What is the appropriate initial principle?', 'Prompt individualized analgesia and assessment of hydration and triggers, with monitoring for complications',[
 ('Routine exchange transfusion for every uncomplicated pain episode','Transfusion is not automatically indicated for uncomplicated pain alone.'),('Delay analgesia until imaging proves vaso-occlusion','This delays appropriate care.'),('Large-volume IV fluids regardless of volume status','Overhydration can cause harm and does not replace individualized assessment.')],
 'Pain treatment should be timely and clinically individualized', '**Use the patient’s effective prior analgesic plan when available.**',['Assess oxygenation, fever and new focal symptoms.','Correct dehydration without indiscriminate fluid loading.'], '**Reassess for evolution into a complicated episode.**',['Acute chest syndrome can emerge during admission.','An atypical pain site or pattern requires an alternative-diagnosis assessment.'],[['Uncomplicated pain','Analgesia and supportive care'],['Transfusion','Not routine for pain alone'],['Monitoring','New respiratory, neurological or systemic findings']],[SC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An HbSS patient develops fever, chest pain, hypoxemia and a new pulmonary infiltrate. Which complication must be urgently considered?', 'Acute chest syndrome',[
 ('Uncomplicated limb vaso-occlusion only','The new respiratory and radiographic findings define a more serious pulmonary syndrome.'),('Stable chronic hemolysis alone','This does not explain the new infiltrate and hypoxemia.'),('Aplastic crisis as the sole explanation','That is characterized by a fall in erythropoietic output rather than this lung syndrome.')],
 'A new infiltrate with respiratory symptoms is a sickle-cell emergency', '**Acute chest syndrome has infectious and noninfectious triggers.**',['It can progress rapidly.','Assess oxygenation, anemia and evolving organ dysfunction.'], '**Treat promptly with coordinated supportive and antimicrobial care.**',['Use incentive spirometry when appropriate and avoid hypoventilation.','Transfusion modality depends on severity and baseline Hb.'],[['Definition clue','New infiltrate plus respiratory syndrome'],['Causes','Infection, fat embolic and vaso-occlusive processes'],['Urgency','Can deteriorate rapidly']],[SC,TR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with sickle-cell disease has rapidly progressive severe acute chest syndrome with escalating oxygen need. Which transfusion strategy is favored by ASH in an appropriate setting?', 'Red-cell exchange rather than simple transfusion alone',[
 ('No transfusion because pulmonary disease is unrelated to HbS','Reducing the sickle-cell burden can be important.'),('Unrestricted simple transfusion to Hb 15 g/dL','Excess viscosity is a concern.'),('Platelet transfusion as a substitute for red-cell exchange','This does not reduce the HbS red-cell fraction.')],
 'Severe acute chest syndrome can require rapid HbS reduction', '**ASH conditionally favors automated or manual exchange in severe ACS.**',['Automated exchange can reduce HbS more rapidly when available.','Simple transfusion may be appropriate in less severe disease or as an urgent bridge in selected anemia.'], '**Coordinate hematology, transfusion medicine and critical care.**',['Use appropriate antigen matching.','Monitor respiratory trajectory and postprocedure Hb and fractions.'],[['Severe / progressive ACS','Exchange assessment'],['Moderate disease','Simple or exchange depending on context'],['Safety','Avoid hyperviscosity and alloimmunization']],[TR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A child with HbSS has abrupt severe anemia and a markedly reduced reticulocyte count after a viral illness, without a newly enlarged spleen. Which complication is most likely?', 'Transient aplastic crisis, commonly due to parvovirus B19',[
 ('Splenic sequestration','That usually has acute splenic enlargement and a preserved or increased marrow response.'),('An uncomplicated hemolytic episode with adequate regeneration','The very low reticulocytes indicate production failure.'),('Iron overload as the immediate explanation','It does not explain the abrupt reticulocytopenic crisis.')],
 'Short red-cell survival makes a temporary production pause clinically important', '**Parvovirus can suppress erythroid precursors.**',['The Hb can fall quickly in a chronic hemolytic disorder.','Other lineages may be less prominently affected.'], '**Provide support while assessing severity and infection context.**',['Transfusion may be needed.','Persistent infection is a different concern in immunocompromised patients.'],[['Aplastic crisis','Low reticulocytes'],['Sequestration','Enlarged spleen with pooling'],['Mechanism','Temporary erythroid suppression']],[SC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A young child with HbSS develops sudden pallor, shock, a rapidly enlarging spleen and a major Hb fall with reticulocytosis. Which diagnosis best fits?', 'Acute splenic sequestration',[
 ('Parvovirus-associated aplastic crisis','Reticulocytopenia rather than reticulocytosis is typical.'),('Stable functional asplenia','That does not explain acute splenic enlargement and pooling.'),('An isolated iron-deficiency episode','It does not cause this abrupt circulatory collapse pattern.')],
 'Splenic pooling can create an acute volume and red-cell emergency', '**An enlarged spleen with acute Hb decline and shock is a characteristic pattern.**',['Platelets can also be sequestered.','The marrow can remain regenerative.'], '**Resuscitate and transfuse with specialist monitoring.**',['The trapped cells can re-enter circulation, so avoid overshooting the Hb target.','Teach families how to recognize recurrence and seek urgent care.'],[['Spleen','Acute enlargement'],['Reticulocytes','Usually maintained or increased'],['Risk','Shock from rapid pooling']],[SC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which mechanism contributes importantly to hydroxyurea benefit in sickle-cell anemia?', 'Increased fetal hemoglobin production, reducing HbS polymerization tendency',[
 ('Permanent correction of the inherited beta-globin sequence in every cell','Hydroxyurea is not gene editing.'),('Direct removal of every sickled erythrocyte by apheresis','That is an exchange-transfusion mechanism.'),('Iron chelation as its sole pharmacological effect','It is not an iron chelator.')],
 'HbF modifies the polymerization environment', '**Hydroxyurea can increase HbF and reduce vaso-occlusive complications.**',['Other cellular effects may contribute.','Response and adherence vary.'], '**Use an appropriate monitoring and reproductive-safety plan.**',['CBC monitoring guides myelosuppression and dose adjustment.','Discuss pregnancy planning and specialist advice rather than applying unsupervised interruption.'],[['Key effect','HbF increase'],['Benefit','Reduced sickling-related complications'],['Monitoring','Counts, response and safety']],[SC])
q('Which statement about voxelotor is correct for a current treatment plan?', 'It was withdrawn from the market for safety concerns and should not be presented as a routine available treatment',[
 ('It remains an unrestricted routine option with no safety change','FDA advised stopping prescribing after the withdrawal.'),('Its withdrawal means all sickle-cell treatments were withdrawn','Other disease-modifying and supportive options remain.'),('A prior Hb rise proves the overall benefit-risk balance must be favorable','A surrogate improvement does not override adverse clinical outcomes.')],
 'A historically approved mechanism is not necessarily a current option', '**Voxelotor withdrawal followed concerning clinical outcome data.**',['The safety signal included more vaso-occlusive events in relevant postmarketing trials.','FDA advised clinicians to stop prescribing it.'], '**Review alternatives through the treating sickle-cell service.**',['Do not leave patients without a transition plan.','Keep educational drug lists synchronized with regulatory changes.'],[['Historical drug','Voxelotor'],['Current issue','Withdrawal due to safety concerns'],['Action','Use an appropriate alternative treatment plan']],[VO])
q('A patient with HbSS develops acute focal neurological deficits. What is the appropriate principle?', 'Urgent stroke evaluation with prompt hematology-led transfusion assessment, commonly exchange, alongside neurological care',[
 ('Observe until the deficits have lasted 24 hours','Delay can worsen neurological injury.'),('Treat only with oral analgesics because all deficits are pain-related','A new focal deficit is an emergency.'),('Avoid all transfusion because HbSS blood is always hyperviscous','The transfusion strategy is designed to improve oxygen delivery and reduce HbS while managing viscosity.')],
 'Stroke is a time-critical sickle-cell complication', '**Assess ischemia, hemorrhage and other causes urgently.**',['Imaging and specialist pathways should proceed promptly.','Do not postpone all action for delayed complete investigations.'], '**Select the transfusion method for the clinical and hematological context.**',['Exchange reduces HbS without an excessive total Hb increase.','Severe anemia or delayed exchange availability may require an individualized bridge strategy.'],[['Immediate','Neurological emergency assessment'],['Hematological goal','Improve delivery and reduce HbS'],['Method','Specialist simple/exchange decision']],[SC,TR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why is extended antigen profiling and appropriate matching important before repeated transfusions in sickle-cell disease?', 'It reduces alloimmunization risk and helps future compatibility management',[
 ('ABO typing alone prevents every clinically important antibody','Other red-cell antigens are important.'),('Matching eliminates all delayed hemolytic reactions','Risk is reduced but not abolished.'),('An antibody history can be ignored once its current screen is negative','Previously detected antibodies remain clinically relevant.')],
 'A long transfusion history creates cumulative immunohematological risk', '**ASH recommends early antigen profiling and prophylactic Rh/K matching beyond ABO/RhD alone.**',['Genotyping can be useful, particularly after recent transfusion.','Preserve and share the antibody history across services.'], '**Recognize delayed hemolysis and hyperhemolysis.**',['An unexplained post-transfusion Hb fall needs urgent review.','Avoid casually repeating transfusions without specialist assessment when hyperhemolysis is possible.'],[['Before exposure','Extended profile'],['Matching','Rh and K with further needs individualized'],['Long-term','Maintain antibody history']],[TR])
q('How does exagamglogene autotemcel increase HbF?', 'Ex vivo CRISPR editing of the erythroid BCL11A enhancer in autologous stem cells',[
 ('Direct replacement of all mature circulating red cells every day','This is not the cellular product mechanism.'),('Inhibition of C1s complement','That describes sutimlimab.'),('Transient iron chelation during an infusion','This is unrelated to the edited-cell mechanism.')],
 'Editing an HbF repressor can alter subsequent erythropoiesis', '**The erythroid enhancer edit reduces BCL11A expression in the relevant lineage.**',['This permits increased fetal hemoglobin production.','The approach uses collected autologous hematopoietic cells.'], '**Assess the complete treatment pathway and risks.**',['Conditioning, engraftment, fertility and long-term safety need discussion.','Eligibility must use the current local product label rather than an old age cutoff.'],[['Target','Erythroid BCL11A enhancer'],['Platform','Ex vivo edited autologous stem cells'],['Effect','Increased HbF']],[GE])
q('Which statement best compares allogeneic transplantation and autologous gene-based treatment in sickle-cell disease?', 'Both can offer major disease-modifying potential in selected patients but require specialist assessment of distinct risks and treatment burdens',[
 ('Both are simple outpatient pills with no conditioning risk','They are complex cellular treatment pathways.'),('Autologous treatment requires an HLA-matched donor in every case','It uses the patient’s own collected cells.'),('Allogeneic transplantation has no graft-versus-host disease risk','GVHD is an important allogeneic risk.')],
 'Potentially transformative treatment still requires an individualized decision', '**Donor availability, organ function, age and disease burden influence the choice.**',['Allogeneic and autologous strategies have different immunological risks.','Conditioning-related toxicity can be relevant to both.'], '**Discuss fertility, long-term follow-up and alternatives.**',['Avoid promising universal cure without risk.','Continue appropriate supportive care throughout evaluation and treatment.'],[['Allogeneic','Donor and GVHD considerations'],['Autologous gene-based','Manufacturing and product-specific risks'],['Shared issues','Conditioning and long-term follow-up']],[GE,SC])
